Shoals.Stochastic: merton jump intensity is too large to enumerate the jump count exactly
Assumption: the enumerated jump-count table is sized on the exponentially
tilted mean lambda * t * exp(jump_mean + 0.5 * jump_vol^2), because that is
where the terms of E[exp(J)] peak. A caller asking for an intensity whose
table would exceed the slot cap gets a refusal naming that quantity, not a
silently truncated jump law and not a multi-hundred-megabyte allocation.

The cap is a cost bound rather than a model bound, and it is the jump MEAN that
breaches it here: at a zero jump mean the tilt is one, so the same lambda * t =
1000 would size the table at about 1233 slots, inside the cap. That is why the
message names the tilted product rather than lambda alone.

Raising the cap does NOT make the fixture pass either, and not for the reason
one would guess. Measured with the cap at 1e9: the fixture does not answer and
does not reach its assertion -- it runs until the per-test timeout, 500s in the
run that established this. So the cap is not merely tidier than attempting the
table; attempting it is non-terminating in practice at this intensity, which is
the argument for having a cap at all rather than an advisory.
